{% extends "_base.j2" %}
{% block main %}
<span class="eyebrow">Learn · Real-node observability</span>
<h1>Measurements</h1>
<p class="lede">Measurements are reusable taps that attach to a version-pinned real-node scenario. They explain node and protocol behavior without replacing the security workload or turning DWARF into a simulator.</p>

<p class="definition-actions"><a class="cta cta--primary" href="/operate/measurements">Browse measurements</a><a class="cta cta--ghost" href="/operate/measurement-profiles">Browse profiles</a></p>
<p><a href="/learn/measurement-coverage">Open measurement coverage</a> to see which taps apply to each threat, risk, scenario family, and node surface, and which retained runs exercise them.</p>

<hr class="hairline">

<section id="definitions">
  <span class="eyebrow">Model</span>
  <h2>Tap, profile, scenario, evidence</h2>
  <div class="tile-grid">
    <article class="tile"><span class="tile__eyebrow">Measurement</span><span class="tile__metric tile__metric--label">one named signal</span><span class="tile__sub">Defines compatibility, source, collector lifecycle, units, correlation IDs, artifacts, and optional threshold behavior.</span></article>
    <article class="tile"><span class="tile__eyebrow">Measurement profile</span><span class="tile__metric tile__metric--label">reusable selection</span><span class="tile__sub">Groups compatible taps for one implementation and target mode. Scenarios may override or explicitly disable them.</span></article>
    <article class="tile"><span class="tile__eyebrow">Run evidence</span><span class="tile__metric tile__metric--label">retained, not inferred</span><span class="tile__sub">Collector state, raw input, normalized events, distributions, reasons for unavailable data, target identity, and reports stay in the run bundle.</span></article>
  </div>
</section>

<hr class="hairline">

<section id="modes">
  <span class="eyebrow">Tap sources</span>
  <h2>Use the least intrusive truthful measurement source</h2>
  <div class="version-catalog">
    <article class="version-card"><div class="version-card__head"><h3>Stock</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--confirmed">preferred</span></div><p>Use signals that the unmodified node already emits, such as traces and metrics.</p></article>
    <article class="version-card"><div class="version-card__head"><h3>External</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--confirmed">independent</span></div><p>Observe the wire, process, container, or controller without changing node source.</p></article>
    <article class="version-card"><div class="version-card__head"><h3>Patched</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--unknown">selective</span></div><p>Use revision-locked node instrumentation for internal boundaries. Claims require exact build provenance and the required stock control.</p></article>
    <article class="version-card"><div class="version-card__head"><h3>Reserved</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--unknown">not evidence</span></div><p>Keep a catalog slot for a future source that is not implemented or selected. Reserved never means available or exercised.</p></article>
  </div>
  <p><strong>Target/build modes are separate:</strong> stock and patched identify target builds. Coverage mode proves revision-qualified code-path execution; it is not a timing source and does not prove a security property by itself.</p>
  <p>Metric definitions can produce a <strong>counter</strong>, <strong>gauge</strong>, <strong>histogram</strong>, or retained timing <strong>distribution</strong>. The report must keep the unit, sample population, correlation boundary, and unavailable reason.</p>
  <p><strong>Catalogued is not exercised.</strong> A definition in the catalog says that DWARF knows how to collect a signal. Only correlated, non-vacuous retained evidence shows that a workload exercised it. See the separate <a href="/learn/coverage">coverage inventory and runtime ledger</a>.</p>
  <p><strong>Child explanation:</strong> A ruler in the toolbox is not a measurement. A saved reading from a real test is a measurement.</p>
</section>

<hr class="hairline">

<section id="interpretation">
  <span class="eyebrow">Interpretation</span>
  <h2>Every terminal outcome keeps its time</h2>
  <p>Accepted, rejected, invalid, duplicate, timed-out, disconnected, and unclassified attempts remain in the combined timing population and in per-outcome distributions. A missing boundary is <code>unavailable</code>, never zero. Resource counters identify whether they describe the node process, its network namespace/container, or the DWARF controller.</p>
  <p>Raw integer nanoseconds are retained when a revision-locked tap supplies them. Collectors prefer that field and derive fractional microseconds without removing the sub-microsecond part. The existing integer microsecond field remains for compatibility, and legacy whole-microsecond evidence remains readable. Measurement revisions are additive: Card 03 keeps <code>whole-microseconds-v1</code>; current Cards 01, 02, and 05 use <code>nanoseconds-v2</code>; Card 04 uses Amaru <code>nanoseconds-v3</code> and Cardano-node <code>nanoseconds-v2</code>.</p>
  <p>Measurements do not change the security verdict by default. A scenario fails because its security assertion failed; a collector error is shown separately. Threshold gating is available only when explicitly enabled.</p>
  <p>A completed run can contain a security finding. Completed means that DWARF finished the run and retained the evidence. It does not mean that the target passed. The failed assertion and its <code>fail</code> verdict remain visible.</p>
</section>

<hr class="hairline">

<section id="reports">
  <span class="eyebrow">Run result</span>
  <h2>What the browser and bundle retain</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>measurements/selection.json</code> — exact profile, tap definitions, compatibility, target identity, collector states, and gate policy.</li>
    <li><code>measurements/report.json</code> and <code>report.md</code> — point values, counts, distributions, throughput, resource cost, and unavailable reasons.</li>
    <li><code>measurements/collectors/&lt;id&gt;/</code> — collector-specific normalized data, samples, correlations, and raw evidence.</li>
    <li>The run inspector — compact status, exact node identity, collector errors, searchable metrics, per-outcome distributions, and downloads.</li>
  </ul>
</section>

<hr class="hairline">

<section id="implementation-status">
  <span class="eyebrow">Current proof status</span>
  <h2>Amaru and Cardano-node are independently proven end to end</h2>
  <div class="version-catalog">
    <article class="version-card"><div class="version-card__head"><h3>Amaru 10.11.20260912</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--confirmed">GUI proven</span></div><p><a href="/operate/runs/20260918T234213Z-64959688"><code>20260918T234213Z-64959688</code></a> retained 100 timed hostile attempts, real protocol and block-application samples, independent sync progress, real target resources, exact source/image identity, 11 finalized collectors, and zero collector errors.</p></article>
    <article class="version-card"><div class="version-card__head"><h3>Amaru patched boundary</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--confirmed">paired proof</span></div><p><a href="/operate/runs/20260919T135417Z-cca4cc8d"><code>20260919T135417Z-cca4cc8d</code></a> proved the revision-locked Handshake instrumentation with exact internal count parity. Matched stock run <a href="/operate/runs/20260919T142302Z-f856f303"><code>20260919T142302Z-f856f303</code></a> used the identical 120-attempt workload and terminal outcomes.</p></article>
    <article class="version-card"><div class="version-card__head"><h3>Cardano-node 11.1.2</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--confirmed">GUI proven</span></div><p><a href="/operate/runs/20260919T032200Z-59f94558"><code>20260919T032200Z-59f94558</code></a> retained 100 timed hostile attempts, accepted and rejected Plutus transactions, stock and patched protocol/block/epoch/VM signals, real target resources, exact build identity, all 12 collectors finalized, and zero collector errors.</p></article>
    <article class="version-card"><div class="version-card__head"><h3>Mixed comparison</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--unknown">deferred</span></div><p>Mixed-node measurement comparison has not started. Both independent workflows are proven, but a mixed comparison remains deferred and requires a separate contract.</p></article>
  </div>
  <p><strong>Observer-overhead envelope:</strong> the paired Amaru calibration uses a 5% maximum absolute delta across mean, median, p95, and p99, with at least 30 samples per leg and exact identity, workload, runner, timing, hardware, and outcome parity. The largest observed delta was <code>0.059686888%</code> across 120 stock and 120 patched samples, so the common externally observed Handshake round trip was within the envelope. This check is informational and non-gating. It does not bound internal hook cost, throughput, saturation, or another workload.</p>
  <p>The retained Amaru devnet uses <code>system_start: 0</code>. Its raw node-reported slot-start age is therefore retained for audit but excluded from comparable propagation-latency claims. Cardano patched values remain revision-specific and require their paired stock control before they are used as performance authority.</p>
</section>

<hr class="hairline">

<section id="five-client-examples">
  <span class="eyebrow">Final retained examples</span>
  <h2>The five frozen cards are complete</h2>
  <p>The accepted labels are <code>framework proven</code>, <code>collection proven</code>, and <code>client requirement complete</code>. Each card has a separate Amaru execution and Cardano-node execution. These runs are not a mixed-node benchmark.</p>
  <div class="version-catalog">
    <article class="version-card"><div class="version-card__head"><h3>01 · CBOR decoding</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--confirmed">complete</span></div><p>Fixed Amaru revision <code>d3a6dafcced78f5809a96619e883cf04911d2bdc</code> run <a href="/operate/runs/20260920T235440Z-050046a4"><code>20260920T235440Z-050046a4</code></a> and Cardano-node run <a href="/operate/runs/20260920T132629Z-ea000d37"><code>20260920T132629Z-ea000d37</code></a> pass the frozen corpus. The old Amaru failure remains a completed security finding.</p></article>
    <article class="version-card"><div class="version-card__head"><h3>02 · Plutus VM</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--confirmed">complete</span></div><p>Fresh Amaru run <a href="/operate/runs/20260921T204537Z-ff5a800a"><code>20260921T204537Z-ff5a800a</code></a> and Cardano-node run <a href="/operate/runs/20260921T202611Z-27eeadb5"><code>20260921T202611Z-27eeadb5</code></a> retain all 60 workload attempts, offered bytes, known outcomes, duration samples, evaluator results, budgets, and real transaction outcomes. The original accepted evidence remains retained.</p></article>
    <article class="version-card"><div class="version-card__head"><h3>03 · Invalid mini-protocol</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--confirmed">complete</span></div><p>Amaru run <code>20260920T072858Z-2cc3bb0c</code> and Cardano-node run <code>20260920T073447Z-ab81bfb7</code> preserve the accepted <code>whole-microseconds-v1</code> evidence and digests. These historical run directories are not present in this checkout, so DWARF does not create dead run links.</p></article>
    <article class="version-card"><div class="version-card__head"><h3>04 · Block application</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--confirmed">complete</span></div><p>Amaru canonical-progress run <a href="/operate/runs/20260921T035546Z-9747122c"><code>20260921T035546Z-9747122c</code></a> and Cardano-node run <a href="/operate/runs/20260921T021935Z-3b58eafc"><code>20260921T021935Z-3b58eafc</code></a> retain canonical progress, raw fork evidence, correlations, and exact timing.</p></article>
    <article class="version-card"><div class="version-card__head"><h3>05 · Restart and sync</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--confirmed">complete</span></div><p>Amaru run <a href="/operate/runs/20260921T045619Z-15e864a0"><code>20260921T045619Z-15e864a0</code></a> and Cardano-node run <a href="/operate/runs/20260921T045807Z-8e2bbb0e"><code>20260921T045807Z-8e2bbb0e</code></a> retain ordered readiness gates, controlled sync ranges, health, and restart-window resources.</p></article>
  </div>
  <p><strong>Child explanation:</strong> DWARF finished five careful recipes with each node. It kept the old problems in the record, and it kept the new proof that passes.</p>
</section>

<hr class="hairline">

<section id="simple-transfer-card">
  <span class="eyebrow">Additive client example</span>
  <h2>06 · Dedicated simple transfers</h2>
  <p>The Amaru run <a href="/operate/runs/20260921T194749Z-26542a57"><code>20260921T194749Z-26542a57</code></a> and Cardano-node run <a href="/operate/runs/20260921T195334Z-e713d0de"><code>20260921T195334Z-e713d0de</code></a> each retain 35 real signed payment attempts: 30 accepted, 5 expected duplicate-payment rejections, and 0 timeouts. Each run keeps every outcome duration, offered transaction and byte rates, exact target identity, target correlation, chain progress, health, and resource samples.</p>
  <p>Amaru protocol-response timing is unavailable because the response came from its support producer, not from an Amaru response boundary. TxSubmission and mempool timing stays unavailable where the selected node taps retained no qualifying event. A zero event count is not proof of exercise.</p>
  <p>Run it from <a href="/run"><code>/run</code></a> with <code>client-example-simple-transfer-amaru</code> or <code>client-example-simple-transfer-cardano</code>. Use the scenario-default deployment and measurement profiles. The CLI form is <code>python3 dwarf/cardano-profile scenario run dwarf/scenarios/&lt;scenario-id&gt;.yaml --backend local-devnet</code>.</p>
  <p><strong>Child explanation:</strong> Each node got 35 real payment tries. DWARF saved every success, expected refusal, clock, and receipt. It did not turn the two runs into a race.</p>
</section>

<hr class="hairline">

<section id="full-metrics-paths">
  <span class="eyebrow">Compatibility audit</span>
  <h2>Full metrics and recommended client paths</h2>
  <p><strong>Configured is not exercised.</strong> A collector counts as exercised only when the bounded workload retains correlated raw or normalized records and a meaningful positive sample, event, delta, readiness result, or timing distribution. A finalized collector, an availability flag, or a zero does not meet this rule.</p>
  <p><strong>Plain meaning:</strong> Putting every ruler beside the machine does not mean that the test touched every ruler.</p>
  <div class="version-catalog">
    <article class="version-card">
      <div class="version-card__head"><h3>Amaru broadest single run</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--unknown">8 / 14 configured</span></div>
      <p>Use <code>client-example-block-application-amaru-canonical-v3</code>, scenario-default <code>profile-y-amaru-block-application-nanoseconds-v3</code>, and <code>amaru-security-patched</code>. Retained run <a href="/operate/runs/20260921T035546Z-9747122c"><code>20260921T035546Z-9747122c</code></a> exercised eight configured collectors. The separate unprofiled coverage definition makes this 8 of 15 implemented definitions.</p>
    </article>
    <article class="version-card">
      <div class="version-card__head"><h3>Cardano-node broadest single run</h3><span class="version-badge version-badge--unknown">8 / 12 configured</span></div>
      <p>Use <code>client-example-cbor-decoding-cardano-patched</code>, scenario-default <code>profile-v-cardano-measurement-nanoseconds-v2</code>, and <code>cardano-security-patched</code>. Retained run <a href="/operate/runs/20260920T132629Z-ea000d37"><code>20260920T132629Z-ea000d37</code></a> exercised eight configured collectors. Three reserved or unprofiled definitions make this 8 of 15 implemented definitions.</p>
    </article>
  </div>
  <p>No one scenario exercises all implemented metrics. Restart scenarios add restart-readiness evidence. Transaction scenarios are prerequisites for transaction and Plutus collectors, but the retained client runs do not contain non-vacuous evidence for every such collector. See the internal technical debrief for the complete 30-definition matrix.</p>
  <p><strong>Plain meaning:</strong> These two recipes fill the most boxes in one run. Some empty boxes need a different test event.</p>

  <details><summary>Exact Amaru /run selections</summary><div class="definition-summary">
    <p>1 <strong>Scenario:</strong> select <code>Client example 04 — canonical block progress — Amaru nanoseconds v3 · devnet</code>. 2 <strong>Target and runtime:</strong> confirm Amaru 10.11.20260912 and devnet. 3 <strong>Profile:</strong> keep <code>Scenario default</code>. 4 <strong>Node versions:</strong> keep <code>Profile default</code>, leave exact-version fields empty, and leave the unconfirmed-version box clear. 5 <strong>Measurements:</strong> keep <code>Scenario or compatible default</code>; confirm 14 <code>amaru-security-patched</code> taps.</p>
    <p>6 <strong>Primitives:</strong> read the fixed canonical-progress operations and assertions. 7 <strong>Run settings:</strong> confirm seed <code>0xB10C0004</code>, iterations <code>scenario default</code>, and <code>Local real-node DWARF engine</code>; allow about four minutes after deployment is ready. 8 <strong>Readiness:</strong> require framework, profile-y, and deployed-version identity. Profile-y is not the current profile. Deploy it through the supported lifecycle before a real run. 9 <strong>Review:</strong> note the current <code>supported-unconfirmed</code> backend label. 10 <strong>Run:</strong> use <code>Start local run</code> only after authorization and readiness.</p>
    <p><strong>Plain meaning:</strong> Keep the recipe defaults, but do not start until the matching Amaru nodes are ready.</p>
  </div></details>

  <details><summary>Exact Cardano-node /run selections</summary><div class="definition-summary">
    <p>1 <strong>Scenario:</strong> select <code>Client example 01 — CBOR decoding conformance — Cardano-node · devnet</code>. 2 <strong>Target and runtime:</strong> confirm Cardano-node 11.1.2 and devnet. 3 <strong>Profile:</strong> keep <code>Scenario default</code>. 4 <strong>Node versions:</strong> keep <code>Profile default</code>, leave exact-version fields empty, and leave the unconfirmed-version box clear. 5 <strong>Measurements:</strong> keep <code>Scenario or compatible default</code>; confirm 12 <code>cardano-security-patched</code> taps.</p>
    <p>6 <strong>Primitives:</strong> read the fixed CBOR and live protocol operations and assertions. 7 <strong>Run settings:</strong> confirm seed <code>0xA7561CD0</code>, iterations <code>scenario default</code>, and <code>Local real-node DWARF engine</code>; allow about four to five minutes when profile-v is ready. 8 <strong>Readiness:</strong> require framework, profile-v, and deployed-version identity. 9 <strong>Review:</strong> note the current <code>supported-unconfirmed</code> label. 10 <strong>Run:</strong> use <code>Start local run</code> only for an authorized real run.</p>
    <p><strong>Plain meaning:</strong> The current healthy Cardano group matches this recipe, but check the fresh readiness result before starting.</p>
  </div></details>
  <p>These recipes use the local backend. Do not use Antithesis or GitHub Actions for these exact plans. Browser review does not start a run.</p>
</section>

<hr class="hairline">

<section id="troubleshooting">
  <span class="eyebrow">Troubleshooting</span>
  <h2>Read absence honestly</h2>
  <dl class="definition-summary">
    <div><dt>incompatible</dt><dd>The tap does not support the resolved implementation, version, mode, or capability set. Explicit incompatible selections stop before workload execution.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>unavailable</dt><dd>The collector ran but the bounded workload did not expose enough evidence for that boundary. Inspect the retained reason and source artifact.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>collector error</dt><dd>The optional tap failed to prepare, start, stop, or finalize. The error remains visible and non-gating unless an explicit threshold gate says otherwise.</dd></div>
    <div><dt>patched-only value</dt><dd>Do not treat it as a production-node performance claim until the paired stock workload and observer-overhead calibration are retained.</dd></div>
  </dl>
</section>
